  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure passing through the region will \nbring showers and isolated thunderstorms late Wednesday through \nThursday.\n\nAn upper trough extending from a closed H5 low over central \nCanada will move through the Great Lakes and into the Ohio \nValley tonight. Surface low pressure associated with this low \nwill stay well north of the area, but the closed low will sag \ndown into the Northeast by Thursday. Behind the departure of the\nsurface low, a cold front slides through the region. Unsettled \nweather on tap during this period.\n\nThe timing of precipitation seems to have slowed down a bit, \nwith scattered showers moving into far northern and western \nzones by midday Wednesday, but the bulk of the showers will hold\noff until Wednesday evening. \n\nShowers will overspread the region Wednesday night through \nThursday morning. While latest models are indicating that most \nof the showers will be out of the area on Thursday, will note \nthat with the upper low over the area and several shortwaves \npassing through the region, unsettled conditions may linger \nthroughout the day Thursday.\n\nWith strong shortwaves, at least Wednesday evening, instability\nwill be marginal, and a few isolated thunderstorms are possible\nin the first half of the night. Not expecting severe \nthunderstorms.\n\nStorm total QPF continues to be on the lower side, generally \nranging from 1/10 to 1/4 inch over Delmarva and southeast New \nJersey, and from 1/4 to 1/2 inch mainly north and west of \nPhiladelphia. Locally higher amounts could fall in any \nthunderstorms.\n\nMild ahead of the system on Wednesday with highs in the low \n70s, then cooler on Thursday with highs in the 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Becoming very warm to potentially hot this \nweekend through early next week.\n\nAs the closed low continues to depart on Friday, a ridge starts\nto build across the East this weekend and into early next week.\nThis along with high pressure centered offshore will result in \nsoutherly flow advecting in a much warmer air mass across our \narea. As a result, high temperatures get into the 70s to mid 80s\nSaturday with widespread 80s on Sunday. Given a southerly wind \ncomponent though, it should be cooler along the coast. The heat \nlooks to build into early next week with even hot conditions \npossible. Highs Monday are forecast to be in the 80s with even \nsome areas reaching 90 degrees (again cooler along the coast). \nGiven the presence of the ridge aloft, no precipitation is \ncurrently anticipated. However near the edge of the \nridge/surface high, there is a slight chance (20 percent) for \nconvection near the southern Poconos Monday afternoon and \nevening. On Tuesday, the heat builds further with highs forecast\nto reach the upper 80s to mid 90s (remaining cooler along the \ncoast). By late Tuesday, there are some signs of the ridging \nstarting to lessen and the surface high moving further offshore.\nThis would result in more potential for convection to develop. \nHowever, this will largely depend on the timing of the ridging \nand surface high shifting away from the area which is uncertain \ngiven this being at the end of the extended forecast period.",
